New Chaffin Discussion Points:
    - Could you explain how you made these pulse shapes match the recorded versions using filters?
    - Pulse shape came from lab right? Xray -> sensor?
    - Can I extend the pulses by just increasing the number the time vector and letting 3rd filter go on...?
    - Maybe clarify that voltage responses are generally expected to be this shape outside of saturation region?
        - Does saturation come from the ADC maxing out?
            - No, from analog electronics (op-amps)
        - If we had a perfect sensor, would we see each voltage response be the same shape with any photon energy?

    - Are there physical effects in the sensor like charge splitting (charge split between neighboring pixels?)
        - Is the voltage response really deterministic based on photon energy?
        - Basically deterministic

    - Do the spectrums come from unfolding of real measurements? Or how were they determined?

    - Does noise floor come from background radiation, or sensor noise? Both?
        In real data, will we know the noise floor?

    - Discussion about metrics
    - Problem with original code: in making traces, trace indeces are set not added (= not +=)
    - Energy:
        - In counting algo, E(triggered) = np.sum(trace[i-5:i-6+int_i]-baseline) * escale
        - In making traces, trace[indeces...] += energies[i] * pulse(magntiude=1)
            if integral(pulse) != 1, does this really preserve the energy?

    ML:
        Straight SL: trace -> energies list
        Auto Encoder: trace -> trace, but bottleneck learns energies
        GAN: A whole new level of complexity...

Vars of interest:
    - count_rate
    - magnitude
    - base_noise (?) -> SNR

Limits
    - noise floor (SNR << 1)
    - Saturation
    - High count rate (but not necessarily coincident index of pulses)
    - P(photons sharing integer index. Harder limit, but may be mitigated with up-sampling)

Metrics
    - % Photons counted (w/ correct index? or by histogram?)
    - % coincident photons in photon list
    - % energy summed together truth_list -> x
    - % Energy counted
    - "FP, TN error", error by scale...
    - Loss. Calculate loss in convolved space. Given some deconv est, re-convolve with prior to then calc error
      problem here is noise in the est can still generate high error... Is that appropriate?

Investigate:
    - HF increase as count rates go up
    - From original scripts, also plot photons that are counted on the original trace (in mV, not keV)

Approaches
    - Time Domain deconvolution
    - F Domain deconvolution
    - ML
        - GAN where A distorts and adds noise to traces. Seems like overkill, plus we might want to know
              distortions and noise we are adding...
        - Convolutional denoising encoder/decoder. Maybe with NAS?
        - iterative method that accepts current estimate of energies + raw signal to produce new estimate of energies?
          This could take advantage of knowledge of integrated energy in the signal, and potentially also probability
          based on the spectrum...
        - Any iterative method is probably going to work better in the pure time domain
        - Extra variables:
            - Prior
            - integrated energy
            - Known distribution of spectrum of photons. How good is this estimation if we cant already count
              well...? If known dist was good, we might be able to create a loss function that could use those statistics...
            - max P(deconv | data, spectrum)

        - Physics informed HPCMP tutorials! Part1: https://training.hpc.mil/course/view.php?id=390

    - Filters
        - LPF. what is Fc? Would need to LPF kernel as well. Chebyshev, Elliptic, Butterworth. Forward-backward?
        - Wiener Filter. What to use as fc
    - At high countrates, convoluted response may appear as HF (relatively) noise. The steady state integration,
      assuming the sensor is not saturated, can give us a limit on the total energy of photons we have received


Future: using sensors with multiple scales: can we use ML to fuse data together?
    - Lower electronic noise
    - Higher precision ADC
    - Can we use multiple pixels with same photomultiplier? Alterate way to improve SNR...

Window functions
    Can we apply these while keeping deconv accurate in the middle of the sample?
    Would help with working with data in discrete chunks...


